# {{.SudoersFile}}: what a brokered command may sudo to.
#
# Written by `faramir init --allow-sudo` and REWRITTEN BY EVERY RUN. Re-running
# init without --allow-sudo removes this file: the grant is the one place the
# executor's reach grows, so taking it away is a re-run rather than an edit.
#
# {{.ExecUser}} is the uid every brokered command runs as. It gets full sudo and
# an authentication step it cannot satisfy by itself: a PAM stack whose auth step
# asks the broker whether a human approved the command making the call. No
# password exists to be typed, stored or stolen.
#
# NOT NOPASSWD, and that is the whole design. NOPASSWD skips PAM entirely, so
# the question would never be asked and anything running as this uid would be
# root for the asking. The PASSWD tag is what makes sudo authenticate, and
# authenticating is what reaches the broker.
#
# THIS FILE IS RENDERED FOR THE SUDO THAT WILL READ IT. Both implementations
# read /etc/sudoers.d, and they do not take the same settings, so init asks
# which one /usr/bin/sudo resolves to and writes what that one parses. Flipping
# the `sudo` alternatives group after an install leaves a grant the host's sudo
# cannot use: `faramir doctor` reports that, and re-running init rewrites it.
#
# The environment is not part of that split. What a brokered command keeps
# across its sudo is read by a pam_env line in that stack, wherever it is, and
# never by this file: sudo-rs has no env_file, and there is no reason to carry
# two ways of doing it. env_reset still discards what the caller had, which is
# right: the
# executor's uid is shared by every brokered command, so anything it was
# holding is a value one of them chose. Not env_keep, which would put that value
# back under the same name.
{{if .SudoRs}}#
# THIS HOST'S SUDO IS SUDO-RS, which has no pam_service setting and reaches the
# service named `sudo` for everybody, so there is nothing to point at a service
# of faramir's own and no {{.PamFile}} on this host at all. The stack is instead
# a delimited block in {{.SudoPamFile}}: a branch that tests the account, then
# the modules that decide, carry the environment and end it. {{.ExecUser}} meets
# those; every other account skips them and meets what the file already said.
{{else}}#
# pam_service is what keeps a mistake here from reaching anybody else: it sends
# {{.ExecUser}} to {{.PamFile}}, and every other account's sudo reads the stock
# {{.SudoPamFile}}, which faramir never touches on a host whose sudo is the
# original.
#
# pam_login_service is the same setting for `sudo -i`, which sudo authenticates
# against a service of its own. Without it a login shell escalation reads the
# stock sudo-i stack and meets a password check {{.ExecUser}} cannot pass, so
# the question is never put and the command fails with nothing naming why.
{{end}}#
# timestamp_timeout=0 turns off sudo's credential cache, leaving the broker to
# decide what an approval covers. It scopes one to the brokered command the
# human was shown: every `sudo` that one run makes, and nothing after it ends.
# sudo's timestamp would instead scope it to the next fifteen minutes, which
# anything starting a command inside that window could ride.
#
# noninteractive_auth is what lets `sudo -n` reach the question at all. Without
# it sudo refuses before the PAM stack runs, so a caller that passes -n -- which
# ansible's become does by default -- fails every task with `a password is
# required` while a human sits watching for a question nothing ever asked. There
# is no prompt to protect: this stack reads no terminal, so the setting admits
# the one authentication method the host has rather than widening it.
{{if not .SudoRs}}Defaults:{{.ExecUser}} pam_service={{.PamService}}
Defaults:{{.ExecUser}} pam_login_service={{.PamService}}
{{end}}Defaults:{{.ExecUser}} timestamp_timeout=0
Defaults:{{.ExecUser}} passwd_tries=1
Defaults:{{.ExecUser}} noninteractive_auth
{{.ExecUser}} ALL=(ALL:ALL) PASSWD: ALL
